Looking at this problem, I need to prove:
```
K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m,k,x),j,y),k) == (if k == j then K.Drop(m,k) else K.Put(K.Drop(m,k),j,y))
```

The functions `Put` and `Drop` are opaque, so I need to use the available lemmas to establish the equality.

Let me trace through the logic:
- `K.Put(m,k,x)` puts value x at key k
- `K.Put(K.Put(m,k,x),j,y)` then puts value y at key j
- `K.Drop(...,k)` removes key k from the result

Case 1: `k == j` - We put x at k, then overwrite with y at k (same key), then drop k. Result should be `K.Drop(m,k)`.

Case 2: `k != j` - We put x at k, then y at j (different key), then drop k. The y at j should remain, so result is `K.Put(K.Drop(m,k),j,y)`.

I need to use `A.Equal` to prove map equality by showing:
1. Keys are equal
2. Values at each key are equal

Let me define intermediate values and prove the keys and values match:

```json
{"HelperA.dfy": "var lhs := K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m,k,x),j,y),k);
    var inner := K.Put(m,k,x);
    var outer := K.Put(inner,j,y);
    O1.PutKeys(m,k,x);
    O1.PutKeys(inner,j,y);
    O1.DropKeys(outer,k);
    O1.DropKeys(m,k);
    if k == j {
      var rhs := K.Drop(m,k);
      assert lhs.Keys == rhs.Keys;
      forall q | q in lhs ensures q in rhs && lhs[q] == rhs[q] {
        O2.DropAt(outer,k,q);
        O2.DropAt(m,k,q);
        if q == j {
          O2.PutAt(inner,j,y,q);
        } else {
          O2.PutAt(inner,j,y,q);
        }
      }
      A.Equal(lhs,rhs);
    } else {
      var rhs := K.Put(K.Drop(m,k),j,y);
      var dropM := K.Drop(m,k);
      O1.PutKeys(dropM,j,y);
      assert lhs.Keys == rhs.Keys;
      forall q | q in lhs ensures q in rhs && lhs[q] == rhs[q] {
        O2.DropAt(outer,k,q);
        if q == j {
          O2.PutAt(inner,j,y,q);
          O2.PutAt(dropM,j,y,q);
        } else {
          O2.PutAt(inner,j,y,q);
          O2.PutAt(dropM,j,y,q);
          O2.DropAt(m,k,q);
        }
      }
      A.Equal(lhs,rhs);
    }"}
```

